          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How do I know whether a three-character category can stand on its own as a usable code?",
          "answer": "A three-character category is usable on its own only if it has no further subdivisions. Most do have them, so you usually have to check.\n\nThe main rule is in the ICD-10-CM code-structure guidance. The three-character form is the category, and characters four to six add detail such as cause, site, severity or laterality. Most categories need one of those extra characters before the code is complete. A decimal point follows the first three characters when more follow, so I10 has no decimal and E11.9 does (coding-icd10-cm-code-structure-and-conventions).\n\nI10, essential hypertension, is the example the corpus gives of a category that is complete at three characters. It takes no further characters, so asking for \"more specificity\" on I10 is not something the classification can provide (coding-hypertension-i10).\n\nThe opposite case is E11, type 2 diabetes. It is never used at three characters. The fourth and later characters name the complication, and diabetes with no complications has its own code ending in 9, such as E11.9 (coding-type-2-diabetes-e11).\n\nIn practice, check the code table for the category. If it lists subcodes beneath it, the three-character form is a header. If it stands alone, it is complete. The sources I searched do not give a full list of which categories are complete, so check the official ICD-10-CM tables for the specific code you are looking at. These sources also describe the US clinical modification. The WHO version of ICD-10 keeps many of the same three-character categories but subdivides them differently, so a code from one may not exist in the other (coding-icd10-cm-versus-who-icd10).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
